uuid "97605f63-4e2b-5abe-a9b2-c8a3d46a4bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-37271" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99904e20-cf2b-59e7-b362-654e7a4f8d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392123} {:answer/id #uuid "b220e044-2814-5fa8-8242-f5a49a8f9977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392124} {:answer/id #uuid "552b06a4-b57b-5fe3-86ff-f0c3f7777811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392125} {:answer/id #uuid "397fa46f-a0aa-5014-b020-3ff9307338ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392126} {:answer/id #uuid "391e7711-d133-5904-84eb-2912d457701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "391e7711-d133-5904-84eb-2912d457701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392127} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6152a7-a6df-58be-b360-b25866cf20a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770a3bd2-95ed-564f-acba-14bf194a130c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37603" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "768e3afb-bb09-5e8d-afce-24265c595101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349537} {:answer/id #uuid "8b60cf77-97aa-5202-8d4e-811565f0e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349708} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea6050c-f1c9-5dd1-b2f0-9331f4c3eb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349731} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e335fc-583e-509e-ace9-504cc6b7c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c9727d-1213-5c42-9fe0-34caddae9641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c93e7214-d507-511f-96e2-a29d77beb762" :question/math-academy-id "q-81210" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0065af71-29a6-5ebd-9bb2-e8f6fd53309c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566} {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} {:answer/id #uuid "20bda32c-1ae4-5791-b78f-9ded45871b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351857} {:answer/id #uuid "43179f12-d2ec-5fea-8ae8-1b1e8c829d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352184} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a0b89-b520-516a-984b-548f08bea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ec5791-f08c-5efc-bb1c-b825849222ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa2ab21a-e8b5-56c0-b8a9-838a56ba099f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86676" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea34ce-51cc-54ce-bd9e-2e2c5ec68b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352535} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac80d5-5537-5ad5-953b-dc791c6b3dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352561} {:answer/id #uuid "7239e333-3699-5e0e-a679-3638ec2f4a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b23a21b-03eb-5d9a-8c5d-1e2dbf0ce327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} 